cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "Value-Field Theory (VFT)"
message: "If you use this theory or repository, please cite this repository."
authors:
  - family-names: "Nuno"
    given-names: "T."
    affiliation: "Independent Researcher"
abstract: >
  Value-Field Theory (VFT) is a structural framework for describing how a
  shared multidimensional real-resource world K, actor-specific accessible
  portions K_i, and actor-specific expectation-forming states P_i condition
  actions and decisions A_i. K is not treated as a separate resource world for
  each actor; actor relativity enters through access to K, through P_i, and
  through actor-specific expected interval changes E_i[Delta K]. P retains
  future outlooks, evaluations, preferences, credit, beliefs, and related
  structures. The term expectation stock refers to this expectation-generating
  role rather than to a stored point expectation. E_i[Delta K] is represented
  separately as an expected change over a specified target and interval, not as
  a required intermediate variable in action generation and not as a rate flow.
  Actions are a principal agentic route for changes in K and P, including
  policy, communication, observation, and information reception. Small
  non-agentic changes in P need not be denied, but are not modeled as separate
  core paths unless they create meaningful observable differences. Economic
  equilibrium, expected surplus aggregation, and real-nominal mappings are
  treated as domain-specific projection candidates rather than VFT-specific
  universal laws. Aggregate expected surplus requires an exchange/accounting
  scope in which resource differences are made comparable and transfers can be
  cancelled. Observable actions, Delta K, Delta P, and nominal-economic
  indicators may be used as proxies subject to domain-specific measurement
  assumptions.
repository-code: "https://github.com/nuno929/vf-model"
license: "CC-BY-4.0"
keywords:
  - value-field theory
  - multidimensional resource state
  - shared resource world
  - actor-specific access
  - expectation stock
  - expected interval change
  - action
  - resource dynamics
  - measurement proxy
  - economic projection
  - social dynamics
